QB.add(35,[{"id":35001,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Which description of a typical virus particle is most accurate?","o":["Nucleic acid in a protein coat, sometimes with an outer lipid envelope","A single cell with ribosomes, cytoplasm and a peptidoglycan wall","A membrane-bound nucleus surrounded by mitochondria, ribosomes and a capsule","Free-floating protein with no nucleic acid at all","A spore containing DNA, ribosomes and dipicolinic acid"],"e":"Viruses are small molecular machines, essentially nucleic acid surrounded by a protein cover. Many also have a lipid bilayer (envelope) around that, which can aid infectivity when the virus fuses with the cell membrane, and surface proteins used to bind specific cells. Particles are 20–900 nm.","w":["That describes a bacterium, which has ribosomes and a wall.","Viruses have no nucleus or organelles.","Viruses contain nucleic acid.","That describes a bacterial spore."],"k":"Virus = nucleic acid + protein coat ± envelope.","rn":[["","What covers the viral nucleic acid?","What covers the viral nucleic acid?","Protein coat (capsid)"]]},{"id":35002,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"What is the usual size range of viral particles?","o":["20–900 nm","1–10 µm","0.1–1 nm","10–100 µm","1–5 mm"],"e":"Viral particles are small, about 20–900 nm (e.g. parvovirus 20 nm, poliovirus 30 nm, HIV-1 120 nm, variola 360 nm, Ebolavirus 80 × 970 nm). Newly discovered giant viruses such as <i>Pandoravirus salinus</i> and <i>Pithovirus sibericum</i> approach bacterial size.","w":["That is bacterial size or larger.","That is atomic scale.","That is animal-cell size.","That is egg size."],"k":"Viruses: 20–900 nm.","rn":[["","What is the size range of viral particles?","What is the size range of viral particles?","20 nm to 900 nm"]]},{"id":35003,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Why can an outer lipid envelope help a virus infect a cell?","o":["It can fuse with the host cell membrane","It produces ATP for viral replication","It contains ribosomes that make viral protein","It stops all antibodies from binding to the virus","It allows the virus to divide by binary fission"],"e":"Many viruses have a lipid bilayer around the protein coat, which can help infectivity, particularly when the virus fuses with the cell membrane. Viruses cannot make ATP or protein themselves; the host cell supplies energy and synthetic machinery.","w":["Viruses do not generate energy; the host provides it.","Viruses have no ribosomes.","Neutralising antibodies recognise envelope proteins.","Viruses do not divide; they are assembled in cells."],"k":"Envelope fuses with host membrane.","rn":[["","What does the viral lipid bilayer (envelope) help with?","What does the viral lipid bilayer (envelope) help with?","Infectivity; Fusing to the cell membrane"]]},{"id":35004,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Into which four general groups are virus shapes classified?","o":["Filamentous, isometric (icosahedral), enveloped, head and tail","Cocci, bacilli, vibrios, spirochetes","Monomers, dimers, pentamers, hexamers","DNA, RNA, protein, lipid","Aerobic, anaerobic, facultatively anaerobic, microaerophilic, capnophilic"],"e":"Virus shapes are consistent and distinct for each viral family and are grouped as filamentous, isometric (icosahedral), enveloped, and head and tail. Examples include the “UFO-type” adenovirus, the bullet-shaped rabies virus and the long, worm-like Ebolavirus.","w":["These are bacterial shapes.","These describe antibody units.","These are molecule classes, not shapes.","These describe oxygen requirements."],"k":"Virus shapes: filamentous, icosahedral, enveloped, head-tail.","rn":[["","What are the 4 groups of virus shape?","What are the 4 groups of virus shape?","Filamentous; Isometric (icosahedral); Enveloped; Head and tail"]]},{"id":35005,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"An unknown sample under the electron microscope shows long filaments with a curled, shepherd's-crook end. Which virus family should this raise concern about?","a":["filovirus","filoviruses","filoviridae","ebola","ebolavirus"],"e":"A long filament with a curled crook-like end is typical of a filovirus such as Ebola (first imaged by EM from the Ebola Zaire isolate in 1976). Ebolavirus measures about 80 × 970 nm. Filoviruses are among the most dangerous zoonotic viruses.","k":"Shepherd's-crook filament on EM = filovirus (Ebola)."},{"id":35006,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Why can viruses multiply only inside living cells?","o":["The host must supply energy, synthetic machinery and small precursors","Viruses need host antibodies to unfold their genome","Viruses can only absorb their nutrients by diffusion through a host plasma membrane","Viral DNA is destroyed by oxygen outside cells","Viruses need host mitochondria to divide by fission"],"e":"Viruses multiply only in living cells: the host cell must provide the energy, the synthetic machinery and the low-molecular-weight precursors for making viral proteins and nucleic acids. The infected cell is forced to produce thousands of identical copies of the original virus.","w":["Antibodies are a host defence, not a replication need.","Viruses do not absorb nutrients like cells.","Oxygen is not the reason.","Viruses do not divide by fission."],"k":"Viruses need host energy, machinery and precursors.","rn":[["","Why can a virus not replicate on its own?","Why can a virus not replicate on its own?","It multiplies only in living cells; Needs host energy, synthetic machinery, precursors"],["","What 3 things must the host cell provide for viral replication?","What 3 things must the host cell provide for viral replication?","Energy; Synthetic machinery; Low molecular-weight precursors"]]},{"id":35007,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Which sequence of viral replication stages is correct?","o":["Adsorption → entry → uncoating → transcription → synthesis → assembly → release","Entry → adsorption → transcription → uncoating → assembly → synthesis → release","Adsorption → uncoating → entry → synthesis → transcription → release → assembly","Uncoating → adsorption → entry → assembly → transcription → synthesis → release","Adsorption → entry → assembly → uncoating → transcription → synthesis → release"],"e":"Replication occurs in seven stages: (1) adsorption, (2) entry, (3) uncoating, (4) transcription/mRNA production, (5) synthesis of virus components, (6) virion assembly and (7) release. The virus gains access, disassembles to release its nucleic acid, which is transcribed so viral proteins take over the cell and new viruses are made.","w":["Adsorption precedes entry, and uncoating precedes transcription.","Entry precedes uncoating; transcription precedes synthesis.","Adsorption comes first and assembly follows synthesis.","Assembly cannot precede uncoating and transcription."],"k":"7 stages: adsorb, enter, uncoat, transcribe, synthesise, assemble, release.","rn":[["","What are the 7 stages of virus replication?","What are the 7 stages of virus replication?","Adsorption; Entry; Uncoating; Transcription / mRNA production; Synthesis of virus components; Virion assembly; Release (liberation); MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":35008,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Name the stage of viral replication in which the capsid is removed so the nucleic acid is released inside the cell.","a":["uncoating","uncoat","uncoating stage"],"e":"After adsorption and entry, the virus disassembles (uncoating), releasing its nucleic acid; transcription to mRNA, synthesis of components, assembly and release follow.","k":"Uncoating releases viral nucleic acid.","rn":[["","What are the 7 stages of virus replication?","What are the 7 stages of virus replication?","Adsorption; Entry; Uncoating; Transcription / mRNA production; Synthesis of virus components; Virion assembly; Release (liberation); MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":35009,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"A patient has recurrent cold sores at the same site years after a primary herpes infection, with no symptoms in between. Which term describes the virus's state between episodes?","a":["latency","latent","latent infection","dormant"],"e":"Viral latency is when the virus lies dormant (latent) within a cell. HSV-1 can spread to sensory ganglia, illustrating how a virus can persist in neural tissue.","k":"Latency = virus dormant within a cell.","rn":[["","What is viral latency?","What is viral latency?","Virus lies dormant within a cell"]]},{"id":35010,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"What single principle underlies the Baltimore classification?","o":["The route by which the viral genome produces positive-sense mRNA","The clinical syndrome the virus causes in humans","The shape and symmetry of the virion as seen under the electron microscope","Whether the virus is zoonotic or human-only","The size of the virus in nanometres"],"e":"Baltimore classifies viruses by their manner of mRNA synthesis: knowing only the nature of the genome you can deduce the steps needed to make + mRNA. The seven groups consider DNA vs RNA, single vs double strand, and the sense of single-stranded RNA. It is mechanistic, not diagnostic.","w":["It is not based on clinical disease.","Shape is a separate grouping.","Host range is not the basis.","Size is not the basis."],"k":"Baltimore = route to + mRNA.","rn":[["","What 3 genome features decide the Baltimore group?","What 3 genome features decide the Baltimore group?","DNA or RNA; Single- or double-stranded; Sense of ssRNA (positive or negative)"],["","What can you deduce from the nature of the viral genome alone?","What can you deduce from the nature of the viral genome alone?","Basic steps to produce mRNA"]]},{"id":35011,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Influenza virus has a single-stranded RNA genome that ribosomes cannot read directly. In which Baltimore group is it?","o":["Group V (–ssRNA)","Group IV (+ssRNA)","Group III (dsRNA)","Group VI (+ssRNA with DNA intermediate)","Group I (dsDNA)"],"e":"Influenza is a negative-sense single-stranded RNA virus, Group V. Negative sense is antisense: the ribosome reads only positive-sense RNA, so the genome must first be copied to + mRNA. Group IV (+ssRNA) is poliovirus; Group III (dsRNA) reovirus; Group VI retroviruses; Group I adenovirus/HSV.","w":["+ssRNA (poliovirus) can be read directly.","dsRNA is reovirus.","Retroviruses are Group VI.","dsDNA is adenovirus and HSV."],"k":"Influenza = Group V, –ssRNA.","rn":[["","What 3 genome features decide the Baltimore group?","What 3 genome features decide the Baltimore group?","DNA or RNA; Single- or double-stranded; Sense of ssRNA (positive or negative)"]]},{"id":35012,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Which pairing of Baltimore group and example virus is correct?","o":["Group VII (gapped dsDNA): hepatitis B virus","Group II (single-stranded DNA virus): poliovirus","Group IV (+ssRNA): reovirus","Group I (dsDNA): parvovirus","Group V (–ssRNA): retrovirus"],"e":"Group I dsDNA (adenovirus, HSV); II ssDNA (parvovirus); III dsRNA (reovirus); IV +ssRNA (poliovirus); V –ssRNA (influenza); VI +ssRNA with DNA intermediate (retrovirus); VII gapped dsDNA (hepatitis B virus). All routes converge on + mRNA.","w":["Poliovirus is Group IV.","Reovirus is Group III.","Parvovirus is Group II.","Retroviruses are Group VI."],"k":"HBV = Baltimore VII, gapped dsDNA.","rn":[["","What are the 7 Baltimore groups?","What are the 7 Baltimore groups?","I: dsDNA; II: ssDNA; III: dsRNA; IV: +ssRNA; V: -ssRNA; VI: +ssRNA with DNA intermediate; VII: gapped dsDNA"]]},{"id":35013,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"What does “positive-sense” mean for a viral RNA genome?","o":["It has the sense sequence, so the ribosome can translate it directly","It carries a positive electrical charge","It is complementary (antisense) to mRNA, so it must first be copied by polymerase","It contains thymine instead of uracil","It is always double-stranded"],"e":"Positive-sense RNA is the sense strand, the same sense as mRNA, so the ribosome can read and translate it; negative-sense must be copied first. It has nothing to do with charge. Once a virus has + mRNA it can make large amounts of protein, helping the virus, reducing host immune responses and shutting down host protein synthesis.","w":["It is not about charge.","That is negative sense.","RNA contains uracil.","Positive sense refers to single strands."],"k":"Positive sense = readable mRNA sense.","rn":[["","What does \"positive\" mean in +mRNA?","What does \"positive\" mean in +mRNA?","Sense strand (read by ribosome); Not charge"]]},{"id":35014,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"HIV is a retrovirus: a +ssRNA virus that makes a DNA intermediate. Give its Baltimore group number (Roman numeral).","a":["vi","6","group vi","group 6"],"e":"Group VI = +ssRNA with a DNA intermediate (retroviruses such as HIV). Group IV is +ssRNA without a DNA step (poliovirus), and Group VII is gapped dsDNA (HBV).","k":"Retroviruses = Group VI.","rn":[["","What are the 7 Baltimore groups?","What are the 7 Baltimore groups?","I: dsDNA; II: ssDNA; III: dsRNA; IV: +ssRNA; V: -ssRNA; VI: +ssRNA with DNA intermediate; VII: gapped dsDNA"]]},{"id":35015,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"A clinician has a patient with a rash and wants to use the Baltimore system to diagnose the cause. What is the problem?","o":["It classifies mechanism of mRNA production and cannot diagnose a virus","It only applies to bacteria, not viruses","It requires electron microscopy of every clinical sample before it can be applied","It only covers DNA viruses","It has been replaced by Koch's postulates"],"e":"Baltimore is mechanistic, not diagnostic: it classifies how a virus gets from its genome to + mRNA so viruses that behave alike can be studied together and antiviral targets understood. It cannot identify which virus a patient has; that needs specimens and tests such as serology or PCR.","w":["It is a viral classification.","It does not require EM.","It covers DNA and RNA viruses.","Koch's postulates link microbes to disease; they are not a replacement."],"k":"Baltimore is mechanistic, not diagnostic.","rn":[["","Is the Baltimore system used to diagnose viruses?","Is the Baltimore system used to diagnose viruses?","No; Mechanistic: it classifies how a virus makes mRNA"]]},{"id":35016,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Why do RNA viruses adapt more quickly to their environment than DNA viruses?","o":["They have a greater mutation rate","They contain more genes than DNA viruses","They are larger and more stable","They lack a protein coat","They cannot be detected by the immune system"],"e":"RNA-based viruses have a greater mutation rate and can adapt rapidly to their environment. This is one reason zoonotic viruses, which are often RNA-based, are so adaptable when they jump species.","w":["Gene number is not the reason given.","Size and stability are not the reason.","RNA viruses have a protein coat.","They are detected by immunity."],"k":"RNA viruses mutate faster → adapt quickly.","rn":[["","Why can RNA viruses adapt rapidly to their environment?","Why can RNA viruses adapt rapidly to their environment?","Greater mutation rate"]]},{"id":35017,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"Viral pathogenesis has two components. Which pair is correct?","o":["Effects of viral replication on the host, and effects of the host response","Effects of antibiotics, and effects of vaccines","Viral shape, and viral size","The route by which the virus was transmitted, and the method used to diagnose it","Baltimore group, and genome length"],"e":"Viral pathogenesis is the processes by which viral infection results in disease. Its two components are the effects of virus replication on the host and the effects of the host response on the virus and the host. Studies aim to identify viral and host genes that influence disease, often using animal models.","w":["These are interventions, not pathogenesis.","These are structural features.","These are epidemiology and testing.","These are classification features."],"k":"Pathogenesis = replication effects + host response effects.","rn":[["","What are the 2 components of viral disease?","What are the 2 components of viral disease?","Effects of virus replication on the host; Effects of host response on virus and host"]]},{"id":35018,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"Which set lists the three requirements for a successful viral infection?","o":["Sufficient virus; accessible, susceptible, permissive cells; local defence absent or overcome","A cell wall; a capsule; spores","A vector; a nucleus; mitochondria","A high fever, a widespread skin rash and enlarged lymph nodes, all present before the virus arrives","High temperature; low pH; high salt"],"e":"A successful infection needs sufficient virus, cells that are accessible, susceptible and permissive, and local antiviral defence that is absent or overcome. The dose needed varies: in one experiment Ebola Zaire infected with as few as 10–50 virions, whereas influenza needs more than 300.","w":["These are bacterial features.","Viruses need host cells, not these.","These are clinical signs.","These are physical growth conditions for bacteria."],"k":"Three requirements: dose, suitable cells, defences overcome.","rn":[["","What are the 3 requirements for a successful infection?","What are the 3 requirements for a successful infection?","Sufficient virus; Cells accessible, susceptible, permissive; Local antiviral defence absent or overcome"]]},{"id":35019,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"Which statement about infectious dose is supported by the lecture?","o":["Ebola Zaire infected with as few as 10–50 virions, while influenza needs over 300","All viruses need exactly the same number of particles","Influenza needs only a single virion, whereas Ebola Zaire needs many thousands of virions","Infectious dose depends only on the host's age","Viral dose is irrelevant if cells are permissive"],"e":"“Sufficient virus” differs between viruses: in a laboratory experiment the Ebola Zaire strain infected a host with as few as 10–50 virions, whereas influenza needs over 300. Dose is one of three requirements, alongside suitable cells and overcoming defences.","w":["Doses differ between viruses.","The numbers are reversed.","Dose depends on the virus.","Sufficient virus is still required."],"k":"Infectious dose varies (Ebola 10–50; flu >300).","rn":[["","How many Ebola Zaire virions can infect a host?","How many Ebola Zaire virions can infect a host?","As few as 10 to 50"],["","How many influenza virions are needed to infect a host?","How many influenza virions are needed to infect a host?","Over 300 (varies by virus)"]]},{"id":35020,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"A nurse with influenza works a shift and a patient on her ward develops influenza. Which term specifically describes infection of a patient caused by a health care worker's activity?","o":["Iatrogenic","Vertical","Zoonotic","Germ line","Horizontal only"],"e":"Iatrogenic infection: activity of a health care worker leads to infection of a patient. Nosocomial describes an individual infected while in hospital or a health care facility. Vertical is parent to offspring; zoonotic is between species; germline is transmission as part of the genome.","w":["Vertical is parent to offspring.","Zoonotic is between species.","Germline is via the genome.","Horizontal is within a species and is less specific than iatrogenic."],"k":"Health worker → patient = iatrogenic.","rn":[["","What is Iatrogenic transmission?","What is Iatrogenic transmission?","Activity of health care worker leads to infection of patient"]]},{"id":35021,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"A baby is born HIV-positive to a mother with untreated HIV. What type of transmission is this?","a":["vertical","vertical transmission","mother to child","mother-to-child"],"e":"Vertical transmission is transfer of infection between parent and offspring, e.g. HIV infecting a fetus or child. Horizontal is between members of the same species; zoonotic between species; germline means the agent is carried in the genome (very rare).","k":"Parent → offspring = vertical.","rn":[["","What is Vertical transmission?","What is Vertical transmission?","Infection passed between parent and offspring"],["","Give an example of vertical transmission of a virus.","Give an example of vertical transmission of a virus.","HIV: infection of a child or fetus from the parent"]]},{"id":35022,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"Which pairing of transmission term and definition is correct?","o":["Germ line: the agent is transmitted as part of the genome","Horizontal: between members of different species","Zoonotic: between members of the same species","Nosocomial: acquired through a procedure carried out by a health worker","Vertical: between unrelated adults"],"e":"Horizontal: same species. Zoonotic: different species (e.g. bats to humans). Iatrogenic: health worker activity infects a patient. Nosocomial: infection while in hospital or a health care facility. Vertical: parent to offspring. Germ line: agent transmitted as part of the genome; about 8–10% of our genome is viral in origin from evolution.","w":["Horizontal is within a species.","Zoonotic is between species.","That is iatrogenic.","Vertical is parent to offspring."],"k":"Germline transmission = carried in the genome.","rn":[["","What is Germ line transmission?","What is Germ line transmission?","Agent transmitted as part of the genome"]]},{"id":35023,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"Approximately what fraction of the human genome is of viral origin from evolution?","o":["About 8–10%","Less than 0.1%","About 50%","About 90%","None"],"e":"Roughly 8–10% of the genome is viral, acquired during evolution (germline transmission). It no longer seems to do anything and is scattered randomly rather than in one block.","w":["Much more than this.","Too high.","Far too high.","Some of the genome is viral."],"k":"~8–10% of our genome is viral.","rn":[["","What proportion of the human genome is viral in origin?","What proportion of the human genome is viral in origin?","About 8 to 10%; Scattered through the genome"]]},{"id":35024,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which is the most common route of viral entry?","o":["Respiratory tract","Alimentary (gut) tract","Urogenital tract","Eye","Skin"],"e":"The respiratory tract is the most common route: viruses enter in aerosolised droplets from coughs or sneezes or by contact with saliva. We must all breathe, the absorptive area is large (140 m²) with ventilation of 6 L/min, and aerosols spread efficiently. Barriers include swallowing, ciliary action and alveolar macrophages.","w":["A route, but not the most common.","Protected by mucus and low pH.","Washed by the eyelid.","The dead outer layer blocks infection."],"k":"Respiratory tract = commonest viral entry route.","rn":[["","What is the most common route of viral entry?","What is the most common route of viral entry?","Respiratory tract"]]},{"id":35025,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Where do large and small respiratory droplets tend to lodge?","o":["Large in the nose; smaller in the airways or alveoli","Large in the alveoli; smaller ones deposited in the nose and throat","All in the stomach after swallowing","Large in the conjunctiva; small in the skin","All are cleared before reaching any tissue"],"e":"Large droplets lodge in the nose, smaller ones in the airways or alveoli. Very small aerosolised particles can travel far and reach the lungs; heavier droplets fall by gravity within about 2–3 metres but can be touched and transferred to the face.","w":["The sites are reversed.","Swallowing is a barrier, not the destination of all droplets.","Not the respiratory pattern.","Many reach respiratory tissues."],"k":"Large droplets → nose; small → airways/alveoli.","rn":[["","Large vs small droplets: where do they lodge?","Large vs small droplets: where do they lodge?","Large: nose; Small: airways or alveoli"]]},{"id":35026,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which is NOT a barrier to infection in the respiratory tract?","o":["Bile detergents","Swallowing","Ciliary action in the lower tract","Alveolar macrophages","Mucus trapping of particles"],"e":"Respiratory barriers include swallowing, ciliary action from the lower tract and macrophages in the alveoli, with mucus trapping particles. Bile detergents are a barrier in the alimentary tract.","w":["Listed respiratory barrier.","Listed respiratory barrier.","Listed respiratory barrier.","Mucus is part of respiratory defence."],"k":"Bile is an alimentary, not respiratory, barrier.","rn":[["","What makes the alimentary tract hostile to viruses?","What makes the alimentary tract hostile to viruses?","Acidic stomach; Alkaline intestine; Digestive enzymes; Bile detergents; Mucus; Antibodies; Phagocytic cells"]]},{"id":35027,"t":"Shedding Host Interactions and Zoonoses","s":"Why is aerosol transmission more dangerous than large-droplet transmission?","o":["Small particles stay airborne, travel further and reach the lower airways","Large droplets contain no virus at all","Aerosols are only produced by the gut","Large droplets travel tens of metres, while aerosols fall within a few centimetres","Aerosols are neutralised by gravity immediately"],"e":"Particle size runs from large to small: large droplets fall quickly near the source (ground deposition), within about 2–3 metres, though they can contaminate hands and surfaces. Very small particles stay airborne, travel much further and reach the nasopharyngeal, tracheobronchial and pulmonary regions of others. Aerosol transmission is the most effective way to infect someone.","w":["Large droplets do carry virus.","Aerosols come from coughing, sneezing and speaking.","The distances are reversed.","Gravity acts mainly on large droplets."],"k":"Aerosols travel further and reach the lungs.","rn":[["","Image card: Large vs small particles: which drop near the source and which travel far?","Large vs small particles: which drop near the source and which travel far?","Large: drop near source (droplet); Small: travel far (aerosol)"]]},{"id":35028,"t":"Shedding Host Interactions and Zoonoses","s":"What mainly determines respiratory droplet size?","o":["The secretions: upper-airway mucus gives larger, wetter droplets; lung secretions smaller ones","The size of the virus particles inside them, since larger virions always produce larger droplets","The Baltimore group of the virus","The patient's blood group","Whether the virus is enveloped"],"e":"Droplet size is not determined by the virus size. An upper respiratory virus produces lots of mucus, so droplets tend to be larger and liquid; from the lungs there is mucus but higher water content, so droplets tend to be smaller. There is a fine gradient in how small a particle must be to stay airborne.","w":["Virus size is not the determinant.","Baltimore group does not set droplet size.","Blood group is irrelevant.","Envelope status is not the determinant."],"k":"Droplet size depends on the secretions, not the virus.","rn":[["","Why are upper respiratory droplets usually larger than lung particles?","Why are upper respiratory droplets usually larger than lung particles?","Upper tract mucus is more liquid; lung particles smaller, more water"]]},{"id":35029,"t":"Shedding Host Interactions and Zoonoses","s":"A patient sneezes into her hand and then shakes hands with a colleague, who later develops the same infection. What is the inanimate-object or hand-contact route called?","a":["fomite","fomites","fomite transmission","contact","contact transmission"],"e":"Large droplets fall quickly but people wipe with their hands and shake hands or touch surfaces; this fomite route allows infection when the virus is then transferred to the face. Nasal secretions contaminating hands and tissues are a respiratory shedding route.","k":"Hand/surface spread = fomite route."},{"id":35030,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which statement about the alimentary tract as a viral entry route is correct?","o":["It is extremely hostile, so only resistant viruses such as enteroviruses infect there","It has no immune activity at all, so any virus, enveloped or not, can infect it very easily","Its contents are static, limiting virus–cell contact","It has a neutral pH throughout","Enveloped viruses can never infect the gut"],"e":"The gut is designed to mix, digest and absorb, so contents are always moving, giving good chances for virus–cell interaction. But it is extremely hostile: acidic stomach, alkaline intestine, digestive enzymes, bile detergents, mucus, antibodies and phagocytes (and many T lymphocytes). Viruses evolved for it, e.g. enteroviruses, reovirus (needs proteases) and enteric coronavirus (enveloped!), resist these.","w":["The gut has abundant immune activity.","Contents are always in motion.","The stomach is acidic and intestine alkaline.","Enteric coronavirus is enveloped yet infects the gut."],"k":"Gut = hostile; only adapted viruses infect it.","rn":[["","What are 3 examples of viruses evolved to infect the gut?","What are 3 examples of viruses evolved to infect the gut?","Enteroviruses; Reovirus (requires proteases); Enteric coronavirus (enveloped)"],["","What makes the alimentary tract hostile to viruses?","What makes the alimentary tract hostile to viruses?","Acidic stomach; Alkaline intestine; Digestive enzymes; Bile detergents; Mucus; Antibodies; Phagocytic cells"]]},{"id":35031,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which virus is noted as a surprising example of an enveloped virus that infects the gut?","o":["Enteric coronavirus","Poliovirus","Reovirus","Norovirus","Rotavirus"],"e":"Viruses evolved to infect the alimentary tract include enteroviruses, reovirus (which requires proteases) and enteric coronavirus, notable because it is enveloped, yet survives bile detergents and the hostile environment.","w":["Poliovirus is an enterovirus, not highlighted as enveloped.","Reovirus is noted for needing proteases.","Norovirus is noted for high infectivity.","Rotavirus is noted for its NSP4 enterotoxin."],"k":"Enteric coronavirus: enveloped yet gut-adapted.","rn":[["","What are 3 examples of viruses evolved to infect the gut?","What are 3 examples of viruses evolved to infect the gut?","Enteroviruses; Reovirus (requires proteases); Enteric coronavirus (enveloped)"]]},{"id":35032,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Why does norovirus spread so efficiently on a ward?","o":["It is highly infectious via the gut, and vomiting aerosolises it","It forms spores that resist alcohol gel and survive on surfaces for years","It is transmitted only by mosquitoes","It needs a living insect vector to survive","It infects only through intact skin"],"e":"Norovirus is very competent at infecting via the alimentary route and very infectious; vomiting also helps aerosolise the virus, adding a respiratory-type spread. Spores are a bacterial feature.","w":["Spores are formed by <i>Bacillus</i> and <i>Clostridium</i>.","Not vector-borne.","No insect vector.","Intact skin's dead layer blocks infection."],"k":"Norovirus: gut infection + vomiting aerosols.","rn":[["","Why is norovirus good at infecting via the gut?","Why is norovirus good at infecting via the gut?","Infects by eating; vomiting aerosolises virus too"]]},{"id":35033,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"How does the urogenital tract protect against viral entry, and how is this overcome?","o":["Mucus and low pH; minute abrasions during sexual activity let viruses in","A dead keratin layer; insect bites let viruses in","Cilia and alveolar macrophages; inhaled aerosols reaching the alveoli overcome them","Eyelid washing; injury and procedures overcome it","Bile and enzymes; enveloped viruses overcome them"],"e":"The urogenital tract is protected by mucus and low pH (which can denature proteins of viruses not adapted to it). Minute abrasions from sexual activity may allow entry. Some viruses produce local lesions (HPV) and some spread from the urogenital tract (HIV).","w":["That describes skin.","That describes the respiratory tract.","That describes the eye.","That describes the gut."],"k":"Urogenital: mucus, low pH; abrasions allow entry.","rn":[["","How may viruses enter the urogenital tract?","How may viruses enter the urogenital tract?","Minute abrasions from sexual activity"]]},{"id":35034,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which pairing is correct for viruses entering through the urogenital tract?","o":["HPV produces local lesions; HIV spreads from the tract","HIV produces local lesions; HPV spreads systemically","Both cause only local lesions","Both spread only via the blood from the start","Neither can infect via this route"],"e":"Some viruses produce local lesions (e.g. HPV), while others spread from the urogenital tract (e.g. HIV). The tract is protected by mucus and low pH, but minute abrasions during sexual activity allow entry.","w":["The behaviours are reversed.","HIV spreads beyond the tract.","HPV produces local lesions.","Both use this route."],"k":"HPV local; HIV spreads.","rn":[["","Which virus produces local urogenital lesions?","Which virus produces local urogenital lesions?","HPV"]]},{"id":35035,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"A swimmer develops conjunctivitis after using a poorly maintained pool. Which feature of the eye normally limits viral infection?","o":["The eyelid washes the sclera every few seconds","The conjunctiva is covered by dead keratinised cells","The eye has an acidic pH like the stomach","Alveolar macrophages patrol the cornea","The sclera is impermeable to all viruses"],"e":"Every few seconds the eyelid passes over the sclera, washing away foreign particles, so there is little opportunity for infection. Infection usually follows injury or ophthalmologic procedures; improperly sanitised swimming pools can be a source.","w":["Dead keratinised cells are the skin's barrier.","Low pH is the stomach's barrier.","Alveolar macrophages are in the lung.","The sclera and conjunctiva are entry routes."],"k":"Eye defence: eyelid washing.","rn":[["","Why is infection of the eye uncommon?","Why is infection of the eye uncommon?","Eyelid washes foreign particles off the sclera every few seconds"]]},{"id":35036,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which virus can infect the cornea, cause blindness and spread to sensory ganglia?","o":["HSV-1","Enterovirus 70","Rotavirus","Hepatitis B virus","Parvovirus"],"e":"HSV-1 can infect the cornea; blindness may result, and the virus spreads to sensory ganglia. Enterovirus 70 is the eye example of disseminated infection spreading to the CNS.","w":["Enterovirus 70 is the CNS-spread example.","Rotavirus is a gut virus.","HBV is blood-borne.","Parvovirus is not described as an eye virus."],"k":"HSV-1: cornea, blindness, sensory ganglia.","rn":[["","What can HSV-1 do to the eye?","What can HSV-1 do to the eye?","Infect cornea (blindness); Spread to sensory ganglia"]]},{"id":35037,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Why must a virus entering through the skin breach deeper than the epidermis to spread widely?","o":["The epidermis has no blood or lymphatic vessels, so only local replication occurs there","The epidermis is too acidic for viral proteins","The dermis has no blood vessels","Viruses replicate best in dead keratinised cells","The epidermis contains neutralising antibodies that destroy all viruses before they replicate"],"e":"The outer layer of dead cells cannot support infection, and the epidermis is devoid of blood or lymphatics, so replication there is local only. The dermis and subdermal tissues are highly vascularised, so infection may spread. Entry is usually via breaks, punctures, abrasions, insect or animal bites, or needles.","w":["Acidity is a feature of stomach and urogenital tract.","The dermis is highly vascular.","Dead cells cannot support infection.","Not the stated barrier."],"k":"Epidermis avascular → local only; dermis vascular → spread.","rn":[["","Why is epidermal infection local replication only?","Why is epidermal infection local replication only?","No blood or lymphatics"]]},{"id":35038,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which is NOT listed as a way viruses enter through the skin?","o":["Diffusion across intact stratum corneum","Breaks, cuts or abrasions in the skin surface","Insect bites","Animal bites","Needle punctures"],"e":"Viral entry through skin usually occurs by breaks, punctures or abrasions; insect or animal bites; and needle punctures. The outer layer of dead cells cannot support infection, so intact skin is a barrier.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Skin entry needs a breach.","rn":[["","What layers make up the epidermis?","What layers make up the epidermis?","Stratum corneum; Stratum lucidum; Stratum granulosum; Stratum malpighii (spinosum); Stratum germinativum"]]},{"id":35039,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Distinguish localised, disseminated and systemic viral infection.","o":["Localised stays at the primary site; disseminated spreads beyond it; systemic infects many organs","Localised infects many organs; disseminated stays in the blood; systemic remains at the primary site","Disseminated means latent; systemic means acute","All three mean the same thing","Localised means in the blood; systemic means in the gut"],"e":"A virus may remain localised, contained by tissue structure and the immune system. Some spread beyond the primary site (disseminated) or infect many organs (systemic). Physical and immune barriers must be breached. Whether a virus stays local depends on the virus and on host defences.","w":["This reverses the terms.","These are not latency terms.","They differ.","Not the definitions."],"k":"Local → disseminated → systemic.","rn":[["","What is a disseminated infection?","What is a disseminated infection?","Spread beyond the primary site"],["","What is a systemic infection?","What is a systemic infection?","Many organs infected"]]},{"id":35040,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"A virus crosses the epithelium. What structure directly beneath the epithelium must it breach next, and what can compromise it?","o":["The basement membrane; epithelial inflammation and destruction","The stratum corneum; sweating","The cell wall; antibiotics","The capsule; complement","The myelin sheath around nerves; demyelination caused by the virus"],"e":"Below the epithelium is the basement membrane, whose integrity can be compromised by epithelial inflammation and destruction. Below it are subepithelial tissues where the virus meets tissue fluids, lymphatics and blood vessels. Directional release from polarised cells (epithelia, neurons) also shapes spread.","w":["The stratum corneum is the outer skin layer.","Host cells lack a cell wall.","Capsules are bacterial.","Myelin is in nerves."],"k":"Next barrier: basement membrane.","rn":[["","What can compromise the basement membrane's integrity?","What can compromise the basement membrane's integrity?","Epithelial inflammation; Epithelial destruction"]]},{"id":35041,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Why does virus in tissue fluid reach the blood readily via the lymphatic system?","o":["Lymphatic capillaries are more permeable than blood capillaries and drain to the blood","Lymphatic vessels contain no immune cells","Lymphatics actively pump virus into the stomach","Blood capillaries are more permeable than lymphatics","Lymph nodes destroy all viruses before they can ever reach the blood, so lymphatic spread is harmless"],"e":"Virus in extracellular fluid is taken up by lymphatic capillaries, which are more permeable than circulatory capillaries, and lymph drains via lymph nodes and the thoracic duct into the great veins. In lymph nodes viruses encounter immune cells, and some replicate in them, spreading to distant tissues.","w":["Lymph nodes contain many immune cells.","Lymph drains to the venous blood.","Lymphatics are the more permeable.","Viruses may replicate in immune cells there."],"k":"Permeable lymphatics → lymph → blood.","rn":[["","Why can virus in lymph easily spread to blood?","Why can virus in lymph easily spread to blood?","Lymphatic vessels are more permeable than circulatory capillaries"],["","What is the path of virus from tissue fluid into the blood via lymph?","What is the path of virus from tissue fluid into the blood via lymph?","1. Lymphatic capillary; 2. Lymph node; 3. Efferent lymphatic; 4. Thoracic lymph duct; 5. Great vein"]]},{"id":35042,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which is NOT a way a virus can enter the blood directly?","o":["Through intact stratum corneum","Through capillaries","By replicating in endothelial cells","Through a vector bite","Via lymph draining into veins"],"e":"Viruses may enter blood directly through capillaries, by replicating in endothelial cells, or through a vector bite; they also reach blood via lymphatics. Intact dead skin cannot support infection.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Lymphatic drainage leads to blood."],"k":"Blood entry: capillaries, endothelium, vector bite, lymph.","rn":[["","What layers make up the epidermis?","What layers make up the epidermis?","Stratum corneum; Stratum lucidum; Stratum granulosum; Stratum malpighii (spinosum); Stratum germinativum"]]},{"id":35043,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"A patient with measles has infectious virus in the blood released continuously from infected tissue. What type of viraemia is this?","a":["active","active viremia","active viraemia"],"e":"Active viraemia results from virus replication (e.g. measles): the virus replicates in a tissue that constantly releases it into the blood (it does not replicate in the blood itself). Passive viraemia is virus introduced without replication, e.g. dengue injected by a mosquito.","k":"Active viraemia = replication in tissue feeds the blood.","rn":[["","What is Active viremia?","What is Active viremia?","Presence of virus in the bloodstream; Results from virus replication; e.g. measles"]]},{"id":35044,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Immediately after a mosquito injects dengue virus into the blood, before any replication in the host, the viraemia is best described as:","o":["Passive","Active","Latent","Germline","Neurotropic"],"e":"Passive viraemia results from virus introduced into the blood without replication, such as a mosquito bite injecting virus as a single event. Dengue starts passive and becomes active once it replicates in the host's tissues.","w":["Active requires replication.","Latency is dormancy within cells.","Germline is genomic transmission.","Neurotropism describes nerve cell infection."],"k":"Mosquito-injected virus without replication = passive viraemia.","rn":[["","Example of passive viremia?","Example of passive viremia?","Mosquito bite introducing virus (e.g. dengue); Not from replication yet"],["","What is Passive viremia?","What is Passive viremia?","Presence of virus in the bloodstream; Results without virus replication; e.g. dengue virus"]]},{"id":35045,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"In active viraemia due to hepatitis C, where does replication occur?","o":["In liver cells, which release virus into the highly vascular liver circulation","Within the plasma itself, with no tissue involved, so the blood alone amplifies the virus","Only inside red blood cells","In mosquito salivary glands","Only in the alveoli"],"e":"Active viraemia does not mean replication in blood: the virus replicates actively in a tissue that constantly releases virus into the blood. A liver virus such as hepatitis C destroys liver cells, and the highly vascularised liver (e.g. portal vein) lets virus enter blood readily.","w":["Active viraemia is replication in tissue.","Not described.","That is the vector in passive spread.","Not the site for HCV."],"k":"Active viraemia: tissue replication feeds blood.","rn":[["","Example of active viremia in liver disease?","Example of active viremia in liver disease?","Virus replicates in liver, breaks down cells, enters blood"]]},{"id":35046,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"In the influenza viraemia curve, why does blood virus titre fall by about day 6?","o":["Neutralising antibody rises and blocks the virus","The virus becomes latent in red cells","All infected cells are converted into spores","The mosquito vector stops feeding","The virus moves permanently into the gut"],"e":"The influenza curve shows blood titre rising then falling by about day 6 as neutralising antibody (NAb) rises. NAbs recognise proteins or glycoproteins on the virion surface and block those that enable entry into cells.","w":["Not latency in red cells.","Viruses do not form spores.","Influenza is not vector-borne.","Not described."],"k":"Rising neutralising antibody clears viraemia.","rn":[["","What do neutralising antibodies recognise on a virus?","What do neutralising antibodies recognise on a virus?","Proteins or glycoproteins on the virion surface; Blocking proteins that let the virus enter cells"],["","In influenza viremia, what happens as neutralising antibody rises?","In influenza viremia, what happens as neutralising antibody rises?","Viremia falls to zero by about day 6; Antibody rises from about day 5"]]},{"id":35047,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"How do neutralising antibodies protect a cell from a virus?","o":["They bind virion surface proteins and block the proteins that enable cell entry","They digest viral nucleic acid inside the cell","They make the virus replicate faster, so it exhausts its supply of host cells and dies out","They convert the virus into a provirus","They destroy the host receptor ACE2"],"e":"A neutralising antibody (NAb) neutralises a pathogen's biological effect: NAbs recognise proteins or glycoproteins on the virion surface (enveloped or non-enveloped) and block the proteins that enable entry into the cell.","w":["Antibodies act outside cells, on the virion.","They do not speed replication.","Not the mechanism.","They target the virus, not the host receptor."],"k":"NAbs block viral entry proteins.","rn":[["","What do neutralising antibodies recognise on a virus?","What do neutralising antibodies recognise on a virus?","Proteins or glycoproteins on the virion surface; Blocking proteins that let the virus enter cells"]]},{"id":35048,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Poliovirus appears briefly in plasma and later rises in another tissue. Where?","o":["Brain and muscle","Liver only","Lung only","Kidney and bladder","Skin and hair"],"e":"In the poliovirus viraemia figure, virus appears briefly in plasma, then later rises in brain and muscle. Poliovirus can reach the CNS by neural, olfactory and haematogenous pathways.","w":["Not shown.","Not shown.","Not shown.","Not shown."],"k":"Polio: brief plasma phase, then brain and muscle.","rn":[["","In poliovirus infection, where does the virus go after the early plasma peak?","In poliovirus infection, where does the virus go after the early plasma peak?","Brain; Muscle"]]},{"id":35049,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"A man bitten by a bat develops agitation and hydrophobia weeks later. The virus travelled from the bite along nerves to the CNS. Name the virus.","a":["rabies","rabies virus"],"e":"Rabies travels up the nerves from the primary site (usually a bite or scratch) to the CNS. It is both highly neuroinvasive and highly neurovirulent; once symptoms appear the probability of death is roughly 99–100%. Bats are a classic reservoir for zoonotic viruses.","k":"Rabies: neural spread from bite to CNS.","rn":[["","How does rabies reach the CNS?","How does rabies reach the CNS?","Travels up nerves from bite/scratch"],["","Rabies virus spreads to the CNS by which pathway?","Rabies virus spreads to the CNS by which pathway?","Neural"]]},{"id":35050,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"During neural spread, what is the direction called when a virus moves along an axon from the nerve ending towards the cell body and spinal cord?","o":["Retrograde","Anterograde","Haematogenous","Olfactory","Vertical"],"e":"Virus entering at a sensory nerve ending or motor end plate travels along the axon retrograde towards the dorsal root ganglion and spinal cord; anterograde spread is away from the cell body. Neural spread can be the defining feature of pathogenesis (rabies, alpha herpesviruses).","w":["Anterograde is away from the cell body.","Haematogenous is via blood.","Olfactory is via the nose.","Vertical is parent to offspring."],"k":"Towards the CNS along an axon = retrograde."},{"id":35051,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"A virus can enter the CNS from a peripheral site but rarely damages nervous tissue. How would you describe it?","o":["Highly neuroinvasive, low neurovirulence (like mumps)","Low neuroinvasiveness, high neurovirulence (like HSV)","High neuroinvasiveness and neurovirulence (like rabies)","Neither neurotropic nor neuroinvasive","Pantropic but not neurotropic"],"e":"Neurotropic: can infect neural cells. Neuroinvasive: can enter the CNS after infection of a peripheral site. Neurovirulent: can cause disease of nervous tissue. Mumps: high neuroinvasiveness, low neurovirulence; HSV: low invasiveness, high virulence; rabies: high both.","w":["That is HSV.","That is rabies.","It enters the CNS, so it is neuroinvasive.","Pantropism is not the issue."],"k":"Mumps: neuroinvasive, low neurovirulence.","rn":[["","Mumps: neuroinvasiveness vs neurovirulence?","Mumps: neuroinvasiveness vs neurovirulence?","High neuroinvasiveness; Low neurovirulence"]]},{"id":35052,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Which term describes a virus that can cause disease of nervous tissue?","o":["Neurovirulent","Neurotropic","Neuroinvasive","Pantropic","Latent"],"e":"Neurovirulent viruses can cause disease of nervous tissue. Neurotropic means able to infect neural cells; neuroinvasive means able to enter the CNS from a peripheral site. Rabies is high in both invasiveness and virulence.","w":["Neurotropic is the ability to infect neural cells.","Neuroinvasive is the ability to enter the CNS.","Pantropic means infecting many organs.","Latency is dormancy."],"k":"Neurovirulent = causes nervous tissue disease.","rn":[["","Neurovirulent virus","Neurovirulent virus","Can cause disease of nervous tissue"]]},{"id":35053,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"HSV infects nervous tissue rarely but causes serious disease when it does. Which combination fits?","o":["Low neuroinvasiveness, high neurovirulence","High neuroinvasiveness, low neurovirulence","High neuroinvasiveness, high neurovirulence","Low neuroinvasiveness, low neurovirulence","Neither neurotropic nor neurovirulent"],"e":"HSV: low neuroinvasiveness, high neurovirulence. Mumps: high invasiveness, low virulence. Rabies: high in both.","w":["That is mumps.","That is rabies.","HSV is highly neurovirulent.","HSV can infect neural tissue."],"k":"HSV: low invasiveness, high virulence.","rn":[["","HSV: neuroinvasiveness vs neurovirulence?","HSV: neuroinvasiveness vs neurovirulence?","Low neuroinvasiveness; High neurovirulence"],["","Mumps: neuroinvasiveness vs neurovirulence?","Mumps: neuroinvasiveness vs neurovirulence?","High neuroinvasiveness; Low neurovirulence"],["","Rabies: neuroinvasiveness vs neurovirulence?","Rabies: neuroinvasiveness vs neurovirulence?","High neuroinvasiveness; High neurovirulence"]]},{"id":35054,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Through which pathways can poliovirus reach the CNS?","o":["Neural, olfactory and haematogenous","Only the olfactory route","Only by vertical transmission","Only through skin lesions","Only through the eye"],"e":"Poliovirus is listed under all three pathways to the CNS: neural, olfactory and haematogenous. Other examples: rabies and HSV (neural), coronavirus (olfactory), measles, mumps and cytomegalovirus (haematogenous). For poliovirus, CNS replication is non-primary.","w":["It uses more than one route.","Not the CNS route.","Not the CNS route.","Not the CNS route."],"k":"Poliovirus: neural, olfactory, haematogenous.","rn":[["","What are the 3 pathways of viral spread to the CNS?","What are the 3 pathways of viral spread to the CNS?","Neural; Olfactory; Hematogenous"]]},{"id":35055,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Reovirus types differ in how they spread to the CNS. Which is correct?","o":["Type 3 spreads by the neural route; type 1 by viraemia","Type 1 spreads by the neural route; type 3 by viraemia","Both spread only by the olfactory route","Neither type reaches the CNS","Both spread only vertically"],"e":"In the CNS-spread table, reovirus type 3 spreads by the neural route, while type 1 spreads by viraemia. Reovirus is an example of non-primary CNS replication.","w":["Reversed.","Not stated.","Reovirus is listed for CNS spread.","Not stated."],"k":"Reovirus 3 neural; reovirus 1 viraemic.","rn":[["","How does reovirus type 1 reach the CNS?","How does reovirus type 1 reach the CNS?","By hematogenous route (viremia)"]]},{"id":35056,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"What is tissue tropism?","o":["The spectrum of tissues a virus can infect","The speed at which a virus kills its infected host","The number of virions needed to infect","The direction a virus travels in a nerve","The genome type of a virus"],"e":"Tissue tropism is the spectrum of tissues infected by a virus. Some viruses have limited tropism (an enteric virus replicates in gut not lung; a neurotropic virus in nervous tissue not haematopoietic cells), while pantropic viruses replicate in many organs, e.g. Ebola.","w":["That relates to virulence.","That is infectious dose.","That is retrograde/anterograde spread.","That is Baltimore classification."],"k":"Tropism = range of tissues infected.","rn":[["","Tissue tropism","Tissue tropism","The spectrum of tissues infected by a virus"]]},{"id":35057,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Which term describes a virus that can replicate in many organs, such as Ebola?","a":["pantropic","pan-tropic","pantropism"],"e":"Pantropic viruses can replicate in many organs (e.g. Ebola), making them especially dangerous; others have limited tropism.","k":"Many organs = pantropic.","rn":[["","What is a pantropic virus? Example?","What is a pantropic virus? Example?","Replicates in many organs; Ebola"]]},{"id":35058,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Which set lists the three determinants of tissue tropism?","o":["Cell receptors, cellular transcription proteins, cellular maturation proteases","Viral size, shape and envelope","The patient's age, biological sex, blood group and previous vaccination history","Temperature, pH and salt","Antibody level, fever and weight loss"],"e":"Determinants of tropism: cell receptors for viruses (HIV-1 and CD4; EBV and CR2; SARS-CoV-2 and ACE2); cellular proteins that regulate viral transcription (JC virus enhancer active only in oligodendrocytes); and cellular proteases for virion maturation (influenza HA cleaved by serine proteases; furin on SARS-CoV-2 S1/S2; the Ebola furin loop).","w":["Structure does not determine tropism directly.","Not the determinants.","These are bacterial growth conditions.","These measure virulence."],"k":"Tropism: receptors, transcription factors, proteases.","rn":[["","What are the 3 determinants of tissue tropism?","What are the 3 determinants of tissue tropism?","Cell receptors for viruses; Cellular proteins regulating viral transcription; Cellular proteases maturing virions"]]},{"id":35059,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"SARS-CoV-2 can infect only cells that express a particular surface protein bound by its spike. Name it.","a":["ace2","angiotensin-converting enzyme 2","angiotensin converting enzyme 2"],"e":"Human ACE2 binds the receptor-binding domain at the top of the SARS-CoV-2 spike, giving tropism for ACE2-expressing cells: no ACE2, no entry. Furin cleavage at S1/S2 is a protease determinant for the same virus.","k":"SARS-CoV-2 receptor = ACE2.","rn":[["","How does SARS-CoV-2 spike give tropism?","How does SARS-CoV-2 spike give tropism?","Spike proteins binds ACE2; Infects cells that express ACE2; no ACE2, no entry"],["","What is the cell receptor for SARS-CoV-2?","What is the cell receptor for SARS-CoV-2?","ACE2"],["","Which SARS-CoV-2 protein binds ACE2?","Which SARS-CoV-2 protein binds ACE2?","Spike protein"]]},{"id":35060,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"JC papovavirus replicates only in oligodendrocytes. Which determinant of tropism explains this?","o":["Its enhancer is active only in that cell type","Only oligodendrocytes express CD4","Furin is found only in oligodendrocytes","Oligodendrocytes lack interferon","It is introduced by a vector bite"],"e":"Cell-specific proteins that regulate viral transcription determine tropism: JC papovavirus replicates in oligodendrocytes because its viral enhancer is active only in that cell type. Receptor examples include HIV-1 with CD4; protease examples include influenza HA cleavage and furin on SARS-CoV-2.","w":["CD4 is the HIV-1 receptor.","Furin is the SARS-CoV-2 protease example, not JC virus.","Not the stated mechanism.","Not vector-borne."],"k":"JC virus tropism via cell-specific transcription.","rn":[["","Why does JC papovavirus replicate in oligodendrocytes?","Why does JC papovavirus replicate in oligodendrocytes?","Viral enhancer only active there"]]},{"id":35061,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"HIV-1 infects helper T cells but not hepatocytes. Which receptor-level explanation is given?","o":["HIV-1 binds CD4","HIV-1 binds CR2, which hepatocytes lack","HIV-1 binds ACE2 on the airway epithelium","HIV-1 binds furin on the cell surface","HIV-1 needs serine proteases to cleave its HA"],"e":"Cell receptors are a key determinant of tropism: HIV-1 uses CD4; EBV uses CR2; SARS-CoV-2 uses ACE2. A fall in blood CD4+ lymphocytes is also one way HIV-1 virulence is measured.","w":["CR2 is the EBV receptor.","ACE2 is the SARS-CoV-2 receptor.","Furin is a maturation protease, not a receptor.","HA cleavage is the influenza protease example."],"k":"HIV-1 receptor = CD4.","rn":[["","What is the cell receptor for HIV-1?","What is the cell receptor for HIV-1?","CD4"]]},{"id":35062,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"How does a circulating virus “find” the tissue it has tropism for?","o":["By chance encounters, binding where affinity is high","Target cells release signals that attract the virus towards them","The virus swims actively towards the organ using surface proteins","Neutralising antibodies carry the virus to the correct tissue","The virus is guided by the genome type that defines its Baltimore group"],"e":"It is pure affinity: a random interaction that happens to bind tightly. With heavy infection releasing lots of virus into the blood, the high concentration makes it likely to meet a tissue it has tropism for; with little virus it may be destroyed first.","w":["Cells do not signal to attract viruses.","Viruses are not motile.","Antibodies neutralise, they do not deliver.","Baltimore groups describe the route to mRNA."],"k":"Tropism results from random high-affinity binding.","rn":[["","How do viruses find their target cells?","How do viruses find their target cells?","Pure affinity; Random binding to receptors on cell surface"]]},{"id":35063,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Cleavage of influenza haemagglutinin by host serine proteases is an example of which determinant of tropism?","o":["Cellular proteases involved in virion maturation","Cell-surface receptors that bind the virus","Cellular proteins that regulate viral transcription","Host interferon signalling","Neutralising antibody binding"],"e":"Determinants of tropism are receptors, cell-specific transcription proteins and maturation proteases. Protease examples: influenza HA cleaved by serine proteases, furin proprotein convertase acting on the SARS-CoV-2 S1/S2 spike, and the Ebola furin loop.","w":["Receptor examples are CD4, CR2 and ACE2.","The transcription example is the JC virus enhancer.","Interferon is a host defence.","Antibodies block entry; they do not define tropism."],"k":"HA cleavage = protease determinant of tropism.","rn":[["","Which cellular protease examples help virion maturation?","Which cellular protease examples help virion maturation?","Serine proteases cleave influenza HA; Furin cleaves SARS-CoV-2 S1/S2 and Ebola furin loop"],["","What are the 3 determinants of tissue tropism?","What are the 3 determinants of tissue tropism?","Cell receptors for viruses; Cellular proteins regulating viral transcription; Cellular proteases maturing virions"]]},{"id":35064,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"What does viral virulence mean?","o":["The capacity to cause disease in an infected host","The spectrum of tissues the virus is able to infect","The number of new virions released by each infected cell","The ability to remain dormant within a cell for years","The route by which virus leaves the body"],"e":"Virulence is the capacity of a virus to cause disease in an infected host: virulent viruses cause significant disease, avirulent or attenuated ones reduced or no disease. It depends on the host too: Ebola barely harms its bat reservoir but is extremely virulent in humans.","w":["That is tropism.","That is replication yield.","That is latency.","That is shedding."],"k":"Virulence = capacity to cause disease.","rn":[["","Viral virulence","Viral virulence","Capacity of a virus to cause disease in an infected host"]]},{"id":35065,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Strain X kills 50% of mice at 100 virions; strain Y needs 10,000 virions. Which statement is correct?","o":["X is more virulent: its LD50 is lower","Y is more virulent because a higher LD50 means a more dangerous virus","They are equally virulent, because LD50 measures tropism rather than disease","LD50 measures infectivity only and says nothing about virulence","They cannot be compared without electron microscopy of both strains"],"e":"LD50 is the amount of virus needed to kill 50% of infected hosts. Fewer virions to kill half the hosts means a more virulent strain. Other measures: mean time to death, mean time to symptoms, fever, weight loss, pathological lesions (poliovirus), fall in CD4+ cells (HIV-1).","w":["A higher LD50 means less virulent.","LD50 is a virulence measure.","LD50 measures lethality.","EM is not needed."],"k":"Lower LD50 = more virulent."},{"id":35066,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Give the abbreviation for the amount of virus needed to kill half of infected hosts.","a":["ld50","ld 50","lethal dose 50","lethal dose 50%"],"e":"LD50 (lethal dose 50%) quantifies virulence, alongside mean time to death, mean time to symptoms, fever, weight loss, lesions and CD4+ lymphocyte reduction (HIV-1).","k":"LD50 = dose killing 50% of hosts.","rn":[["","LD50","LD50","Amount of virus needed to kill 50% of infected hosts"],["","What are the 5 ways to quantitate virulence?","What are the 5 ways to quantitate virulence?","LD50; Mean time to death; Mean time to symptoms; Fever or weight loss; Pathological lesions / CD4+ fall"]]},{"id":35067,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"Which is NOT a way of quantifying viral virulence?","o":["The Baltimore group number","LD50","Mean time to death","Mean time to appearance of symptoms","Fall in blood CD4+ lymphocytes in HIV-1 infection"],"e":"Virulence measures: LD50, mean time to death, mean time to symptoms, fever or weight loss, pathological lesions (poliovirus), and reduction in blood CD4+ lymphocytes (HIV-1). The Baltimore group describes how mRNA is made.","w":["LD50 is a measure.","Listed measure.","Listed measure.","Listed measure."],"k":"Baltimore group does not measure virulence.","rn":[["","Why does Baltimore classification help avoid memorising every viral protein?","Why does Baltimore classification help avoid memorising every viral protein?","Group tells roughly how the virus works"]]},{"id":35068,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"How are viral virulence genes usually identified?","o":["Knocking a gene out and seeing reduced or no disease","Counting how many different host receptors the virus is able to bind","Measuring virion size and shape on the electron microscope","Testing which antibiotics kill the virus in culture","Gram staining infected tissue from the host"],"e":"Virulence genes are usually identified by mutation: deleting or disrupting one gives a virus that causes reduced or no disease in a specified system. A major goal of virology is to identify the viral and host genes that determine virulence.","w":["Not the method.","Size does not identify virulence genes.","Antibiotics do not act on viruses.","Gram staining is for bacteria."],"k":"Virulence genes found by mutation.","rn":[["","What is the goal of studies on viral pathogenesis?","What is the goal of studies on viral pathogenesis?","Identify viral and host genes that influence disease"]]},{"id":35069,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Which is NOT one of the four classes of viral virulence genes?","o":["Genes that let the virus be seen by light microscopy","Genes affecting the ability to replicate","Genes that modify the host's defence mechanisms","Genes that enable spread in the host","Genes with intrinsic cell-killing (cytopathic) effects on host cells"],"e":"The four classes: genes affecting replication, genes modifying host defences, genes enabling spread in the host, and genes with intrinsic cell-killing effects. Faster replication, rapid immune shut-down (e.g. HIV) and greater cell pathogenicity all raise virulence.","w":["Listed class.","Listed class.","Listed class.","Listed class."],"k":"4 virulence gene classes: replicate, evade, spread, kill.","rn":[["","Why is light microscopy of little use for viruses?","Why is light microscopy of little use for viruses?","Virus too small; Shows only cell pathology"]]},{"id":35070,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Ebola circulates in bats without making them ill, yet kills most infected humans. What does this show about virulence?","o":["It depends on the virus–host interaction, not the virus alone","Bats lack the cells that Ebola needs, so the virus never replicates in them","Ebola loses all its virulence genes while it is inside bats","Humans are infected by an entirely different virus from the one in bats","Virulence is fixed and is decided only by the Baltimore group of the virus"],"e":"Host genes decide susceptibility too. Bats are a reservoir for many viruses; Ebola is not very virulent to the bat but its virulence in humans is extremely high. Virulence is defined by how a virus interacts with its host, not by the virus alone.","w":["Bats are a reservoir, so the virus persists in them.","Not stated.","It is the same virus.","Baltimore does not define virulence."],"k":"Virulence depends on host as well as virus.","rn":[["","Why is virulence not defined by the virus alone?","Why is virulence not defined by the virus alone?","Depends on host interaction"],["","Why can the same virus be mild in bats but severe in humans?","Why can the same virus be mild in bats but severe in humans?","Virulence depends on host interaction; Ebola not very virulent to bat, very high in humans"]]},{"id":35071,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"A viral protein secreted from infected cells mimics a host cytokine and disrupts the immune response. What is it called?","a":["virokine","virokines"],"e":"Virokines are secreted proteins that mimic cytokines, growth factors or similar extracellular immune regulators; viroceptors are homologs of host receptors or cell-surface immune molecules. Both sabotage innate and adaptive defences and are not required for growth in cell culture.","k":"Secreted cytokine mimic = virokine."},{"id":35072,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Which statement about viroceptors is correct?","o":["They are viral homologs of host receptors that sabotage defences","They are normal host receptors that the virus binds in order to enter cells","They are needed for the virus to grow in cell culture","They are antibodies the host makes against viral receptors","They are proteases that cleave the spike protein"],"e":"Viroceptors are homologs of host receptors or cell-surface immune molecules. Examples of defence-modifying proteins: soluble cytokine receptors that bind and block cytokines, proteins binding key complement proteins, and proteins affecting MHC-1 antigen presentation. They are not required for growth in culture.","w":["Host entry receptors (e.g. ACE2) are not viroceptors.","They are not required in culture.","They are viral, not host antibodies.","Proteases are a tropism determinant."],"k":"Viroceptor = viral mimic of a host receptor.","rn":[["","Viroceptors","Viroceptors","Homologs of host receptors or cell surface immune molecules; Prevent immune signals reaching host receptors; Stop immune cells from recognizing infected ones"],["","What are 3 examples of genes modifying host defence?","What are 3 examples of genes modifying host defence?","Soluble cytokine receptor; Complement-binding proteins; Proteins affecting MHC-1 presentation"],["","What are the 4 classes of viral virulence genes?","What are the 4 classes of viral virulence genes?","Affect ability to replicate; Modify host defences; Enable spread in host; Intrinsic cell killing"]]},{"id":35073,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"An infected cell normally releases cytokines as a “cry for help”. A virus releases a soluble protein that binds those cytokines. What is the effect?","o":["It blocks the cytokines, silencing the cell's alarm","It speeds viral entry by acting as a co-receptor","It stimulates neutralising antibody production","It cleaves the viral spike so new virions mature","It makes the cell present more viral peptide on MHC-1"],"e":"Viruses can modify host defences with a soluble cytokine receptor that binds cytokines and blocks their action. Infected cells release cytokines to signal infection; viruses can inhibit or block this. Other examples interfere with complement or reduce MHC-1 antigen presentation.","w":["Not its role.","It suppresses immunity.","That is a protease function.","Viral proteins reduce MHC-1 presentation."],"k":"Soluble cytokine receptors block host alarm signals.","rn":[["","What does a viral soluble cytokine receptor (VSCR) do?","What does a viral soluble cytokine receptor (VSCR) do?","Binds cytokines; Blocks their action"]]},{"id":35074,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Rotavirus NSP4 fed to young mice causes diarrhoea. How?","o":["It raises intracellular Ca²⁺ and potentiates chloride secretion","It is a capsule that blocks phagocytosis in the gut","It is a lipopolysaccharide endotoxin that triggers septic shock in the host","It destroys the basement membrane of the colon","It binds ACE2 on enterocytes to permit entry"],"e":"NSP4, a non-structural glycoprotein of rotaviruses, is a viral enterotoxin. Expressed in cells it increases intracellular calcium; fed to young mice it causes diarrhoea by potentiating chloride secretion, triggering a signal-transduction pathway in the intestinal mucosa.","w":["Capsules are bacterial.","LPS is a Gram-negative endotoxin.","Not the mechanism.","ACE2 is the SARS-CoV-2 receptor."],"k":"NSP4: viral enterotoxin; ↑Ca²⁺, ↑Cl⁻ secretion.","rn":[["","How does NSP4 cause diarrhoea?","How does NSP4 cause diarrhoea?","Raises intracellular calcium; Potentiates chloride secretion"]]},{"id":35075,"t":"Routes of Viral Entry","s":"Which is NOT listed as a site of virus shedding?","o":["Sweat glands","Respiratory secretions","Semen","Faeces","Insect vectors"],"e":"Shedding sites: respiratory secretions, mucosal shedding, urine, semen, faeces, skin lesions, blood, the blood supply, insect vectors, germline and vertical transmission. Shedding is how an infected person passes virus to others.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Shedding sites include secretions, urine, semen, faeces, blood, vectors.","rn":[["","What lies in the dermis and below it?","What lies in the dermis and below it?","Blood vessels; lymphatic vessels; Hair follicle; sebaceous gland; Fibroblasts; macrophages; subcutaneous fat"]]},{"id":35076,"t":"Shedding Host Interactions and Zoonoses","s":"What are interferons?","o":["Host signalling proteins released in response to viruses","Viral proteins that block host cytokine receptors on immune cells","Antiviral drugs that cure HIV","Bacterial toxins released in the gut","Viral enzymes that copy RNA"],"e":"Host cells have intricate signalling networks to detect, control and eradicate viruses; interferons are signalling proteins released by host cells in response to viruses to increase the antiviral response. Viruses in turn evolve to hijack host factors: an ongoing evolutionary arms race.","w":["Those are viroceptors.","Pegylated interferon is used for HCV, but interferons are host proteins.","Not bacterial toxins.","Not viral polymerases."],"k":"Interferons = host antiviral signals.","rn":[["","What are interferons?","What are interferons?","Host signalling proteins; released in response to viruses; Increase anti-viral response"]]},{"id":35077,"t":"Shedding Host Interactions and Zoonoses","s":"From an evolutionary viewpoint, which strategy usually best perpetuates a virus?","o":["Low virulence with high transmission","High virulence that kills the host quickly","Remaining in one host without shedding","Avoiding all host cells","Mutating as slowly as possible"],"e":"Viruses follow Darwinian selection; they are not “trying” to kill. The best strategy is usually low virulence and high transmission: infecting many people perpetuates the viral genome, whereas killing the host quickly limits spread. Host and virus are in a molecular arms race.","w":["Killing hosts quickly limits spread.","Without shedding there is no transmission.","Viruses need host cells.","Adaptability favours spread."],"k":"Low virulence, high transmission favours viruses.","rn":[["","What is usually the best strategy for a virus?","What is usually the best strategy for a virus?","Low virulence, high transmission"],["","Why is a virus that kills its host quickly usually disadvantageous?","Why is a virus that kills its host quickly usually disadvantageous?","Spreads poorly; Best: low virulence, high transmission"]]},{"id":35078,"t":"Shedding Host Interactions and Zoonoses","s":"Which statement about zoonotic viruses is correct?","o":["They are often RNA viruses, which explains their adaptability","They are always DNA viruses with very stable, slowly changing genomes","They can only spread from humans to animals","They never cause severe disease in humans","They are always transmitted vertically"],"e":"A zoonosis is a disease caused by bacteria, viruses or parasites that can spread from animals to humans, e.g. Ebola, bird flu and SARS-CoV-2. Zoonotic viruses are often RNA-based, which accounts for their adaptability, and they tend to be severe.","w":["They are often RNA viruses.","The direction is animal to human.","They tend to be severe.","Not necessarily vertical."],"k":"Zoonotic viruses: often RNA, adaptable, severe.","rn":[["","Why are zoonotic viruses so adaptable?","Why are zoonotic viruses so adaptable?","Often RNA based"]]},{"id":35079,"t":"Shedding Host Interactions and Zoonoses","s":"Ebola was first discovered in 1976 near a river in Zaire (now DRC). What case fatality rate can the Zaire strain reach?","o":["Up to about 90%","About 1%","About 10%","About 30%","About 50%"],"e":"Ebola was first discovered by Peter Piot investigating an outbreak in Zaire in 1976 and is named after the Ebola river; the Zaire strain is one of the most lethal viruses known, with case mortality of up to 90%. By comparison bird flu was quoted at about 60% and the original SARS-CoV at about 10%.","w":["Far higher.","That was the original SARS-CoV.","Too low.","Too low."],"k":"Ebola Zaire: mortality up to 90%.","rn":[["","What is the case mortality of Ebola Zaire?","What is the case mortality of Ebola Zaire?","Up to 90%"]]},{"id":35080,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"A child has a vesicular rash. Which specimen is most appropriate for viral diagnosis?","o":["Vesicle fluid or skin scrapings","Urine only","Brain biopsy","Bronchial washings","Sputum"],"e":"Sample the site of disease: vesicular rash → vesicle fluid and skin scrapings; maculopapular rash → throat, stool and rectal swabs; respiratory → nasal/bronchial washings, swabs, sputum; GI → stool and rectal swabs; CNS → stool, tissue, saliva, brain biopsy, CSF; genital → vesicle fluid or swab; urinary → urine; blood-borne → blood.","w":["Urine is for urinary tract infection.","Too invasive and for CNS disease.","Respiratory specimen.","Respiratory specimen."],"k":"Vesicular rash → vesicle fluid, skin scrapings.","rn":[["","Vesicular rash: what specimens?","Vesicular rash: what specimens?","Vesicle fluid; Skin scrapings"]]},{"id":35081,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"A patient has a maculopapular rash and a viral cause is suspected. Which specimens are listed?","o":["Throat, stool and rectal swabs","Vesicle fluid and skin scrapings only","Urine only","Cerebrospinal fluid and brain biopsy","Bronchial washings and sputum only"],"e":"For a maculopapular rash the listed specimens are throat, stool and rectal swabs (in contrast to a vesicular rash, where vesicle fluid and scrapings are taken). Use clinical features and knowledge of likely viruses to choose samples.","w":["That is for vesicular rash.","That is for urinary infection.","That is for CNS infection.","That is for respiratory infection."],"k":"Maculopapular rash → throat, stool, rectal swabs.","rn":[["","Maculopapular rash: what specimens?","Maculopapular rash: what specimens?","Throat swab; Stool; Rectal swabs"]]},{"id":35082,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"For suspected viral CNS infection, which is NOT among the listed specimens?","o":["Vesicle fluid","Cerebrospinal fluid","Stool","Saliva","Brain biopsy"],"e":"For CNS infection the listed specimens are stool, tissue, saliva, brain biopsy and cerebrospinal fluid. Vesicle fluid is taken for vesicular rashes and genital lesions.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"CNS: stool, tissue, saliva, brain biopsy, CSF.","rn":[["","Genital tract: what specimens?","Genital tract: what specimens?","Vesicle fluid; Swab"],["","Vesicular rash: what specimens?","Vesicular rash: what specimens?","Vesicle fluid; Skin scrapings"]]},{"id":35083,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Why are light microscopy and culture described as old, limited methods for diagnosing viral infection?","o":["Viruses are too small to see, and culture is hard because they kill the cells","Viruses are too large for microscope slides and grow too fast in culture","Light microscopy identifies viruses but culture is too expensive","Viruses stain Gram positive, which confuses the result","Culture always gives false positives for bacteria"],"e":"Light microscopy is pretty useless for viruses because they are too small; you see only cell pathology. Culture is tricky because you are growing a virus in the cells it kills. Current methods detect antibody against the virus, viral protein (antigen) or viral nucleic acid.","w":["Viruses are too small.","It cannot identify viruses.","Gram staining is for bacteria.","Not the problem stated."],"k":"Viruses too small for light microscopy; culture difficult.","rn":[["","Why is light microscopy of little use for viruses?","Why is light microscopy of little use for viruses?","Virus too small; Shows only cell pathology"],["","Why is viral culture difficult?","Why is viral culture difficult?","Virus kills the cells it is cultured in"]]},{"id":35084,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Which three targets do current viral diagnostic methods detect?","o":["Antibody to the virus, viral antigen, viral nucleic acid","Peptidoglycan, LPS, capsule","Spores, plasmids, transposons","Fever, rash, weight loss","Gram reaction, shape, arrangement"],"e":"Current methods detect antibody mounted against the virus (e.g. ELISA serology), viral protein/antigen (e.g. immunofluorescence) and viral nucleic acid (e.g. PCR). Microscopy and culture are the old methods.","w":["Bacterial wall features.","Bacterial structures.","Clinical signs.","Bacterial staining features."],"k":"Detect antibody, antigen or nucleic acid.","rn":[["","What are the 3 current methods of viral testing?","What are the 3 current methods of viral testing?","Antibody against the virus; Viral protein (antigen); Viral nucleic acid"]]},{"id":35085,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"A sputum sample is spread on a slide and labelled antibodies are added; clusters of cells glow green. What does this test detect?","o":["Viral antigen in infected cells","Antibody in the patient's serum","Viral nucleic acid by amplification","Bacterial peptidoglycan","Virus particles seen by EM"],"e":"Immunofluorescence detects viral antigen: antibodies against a suspected virus's protein bind infected cells, and a labelled antibody against that antibody makes them fluoresce. It is quick but needs specific monoclonal antibodies (so not all viruses), a fluorescent microscope and expert personnel; you must suspect the virus first.","w":["That is serology (ELISA).","That is PCR.","That is a bacterial feature.","That is electron microscopy."],"k":"Immunofluorescence detects antigen in cells.","rn":[["","How does immunofluorescence detect a virus in sputum?","How does immunofluorescence detect a virus in sputum?","Antibody to viral antigen added; Fluorescent tag added; cluster of infected cells glows"],["","Image card: What test is this? What do the green spots show?","What test is this? What do the green spots show?","Immunofluorescence (viral antigen); Cluster of infected cells"]]},{"id":35086,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Which is a limitation of immunofluorescence for viral diagnosis?","o":["It needs a specific monoclonal antibody for each virus","It always takes several weeks","It can only detect bacterial infections","It requires a whole-body CT scan","It cannot be performed on sputum"],"e":"Immunofluorescence is quick but not applicable to all viruses: it needs manufactured virus-specific monoclonal antibodies, a fluorescent microscope set-up and expert personnel. The fluorescent tags are added; antigens are not naturally fluorescent.","w":["It is quick.","It detects viral antigen.","No imaging needed.","Sputum is a typical sample."],"k":"IF needs virus-specific antibodies, equipment, experts.","rn":[["","Why is immunofluorescence not applicable for all viruses?","Why is immunofluorescence not applicable for all viruses?","Needs specific monoclonal antibodies"]]},{"id":35087,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"In serological ELISA, which layer is coated onto the plate first?","o":["Purified viral antigen","Patient serum","Enzyme-linked anti-human immunoglobulin","Colour-changing substrate","Viral nucleic acid"],"e":"ELISA layers from the bottom: purified antigen pre-coated on the plate; patient serum (antibodies bind if present); anti-human immunoglobulin (second antibody) coupled to an enzyme, usually HRP; substrate that changes colour when cleaved. Colour = positive; no colour = negative.","w":["Serum is added second.","The second antibody is third.","The substrate is last.","ELISA detects antibody, not nucleic acid."],"k":"ELISA order: antigen → serum → enzyme-antibody → substrate.","rn":[["","What are the 4 layers of an ELISA, bottom to top?","What are the 4 layers of an ELISA, bottom to top?","Purified antigen on plate; Patient serum antibodies; Enzyme-coupled anti-human Ig (2nd antibody); Substrate that changes colour"]]},{"id":35088,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Detecting antibody to a virus in serum or plasma is collectively called what?","a":["serology","serological testing","serological test"],"e":"Detection of antibody (or antigen) in serum/plasma is serology; the commonest test is ELISA (enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay), which can be done in the field within about an hour.","k":"Serum antibody testing = serology.","rn":[["","What is serology?","What is serology?","Antibody (or antigen) detection in serum/plasma"]]},{"id":35089,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"An ELISA well for a patient stays colourless. What is the correct interpretation?","o":["No specific antibody bound, so the test is negative","The patient has very high antibody levels","The enzyme was added before the antigen","The virus is present but not replicating","The test detects nucleic acid, which was absent"],"e":"If patient antibodies bind the coated antigen, the enzyme-linked second antibody binds them and cleaves the substrate, changing colour (positive). No colour means no specific antibody bound: negative.","w":["High antibody would give colour.","Not the interpretation of a properly run test.","ELISA does not assess replication.","ELISA detects antibody (or antigen)."],"k":"No colour = negative ELISA.","rn":[["","Why is immunofluorescence not applicable for all viruses?","Why is immunofluorescence not applicable for all viruses?","Needs specific monoclonal antibodies"],["","What are the 3 current methods of viral testing?","What are the 3 current methods of viral testing?","Antibody against the virus; Viral protein (antigen); Viral nucleic acid"]]},{"id":35090,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Which enzyme is usually coupled to the second antibody in ELISA?","a":["hrp","horseradish peroxidase","peroxidase"],"e":"The coupled enzyme is usually HRP (horseradish peroxidase), which reacts with the substrate dye to produce a visible colour change in positive wells.","k":"ELISA enzyme: HRP.","rn":[["","What enzyme is usually coupled in ELISA?","What enzyme is usually coupled in ELISA?","HRP"]]},{"id":35091,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"A febrile returning traveller could have one of several known viruses. Which tool runs many serological tests for different viruses at once in hospital?","o":["A random access analyser","An electron microscope looking for particles","A Gram-stained blood film under light microscopy","One immunofluorescence slide for a single virus","Baltimore classification of the presentation"],"e":"A random access analyser runs multiple serological tests for different viruses simultaneously, in about a couple of hours. It suits diagnostic uncertainty but only tests for viruses you suspect (known viruses).","w":["EM is reserved for unknown viruses.","Light microscopy cannot see viruses.","Immunofluorescence tests one suspected virus.","Baltimore is not diagnostic."],"k":"Random access analyser: many serology tests at once.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this machine? What does it do?","What is this machine? What does it do?","Random access analyser; Multiple serological tests for different viruses at once"],["","What does a random access analyser do?","What does a random access analyser do?","Multiple serological tests for different viruses simultaneously"]]},{"id":35092,"t":"Shedding Host Interactions and Zoonoses","s":"What is the main limitation of the random access analyser?","o":["It only detects viruses already suspected","Results take several weeks to come back","It needs a brain biopsy for every run","It detects bacteria rather than viruses","It can test only one virus per run"],"e":"It tests multiple known viruses quickly, but only those you suspect. An unknown virus needs culture, electron microscopy and sequencing.","w":["It is fast.","It uses serum.","It tests for viruses.","It runs many tests at once."],"k":"Analyser finds only suspected viruses.","rn":[["","What have host cells developed against viruses?","What have host cells developed against viruses?","Signalling networks to detect, control, eradicate"]]},{"id":35093,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"A patient has very low levels of a virus that cannot be grown in culture. Which test suits best?","o":["PCR for viral nucleic acid","Light microscopy of a blood film","Viral culture in cell lines","Gram stain of the sputum sample","Immunofluorescence with no specific antibody"],"e":"Nucleic acid detection works for all viruses but is especially helpful for non-cultivable viruses and low copy numbers. PCR amplifies logarithmically, gives results within 24 hours (field kits in a couple of hours) and can quantify viral load.","w":["Viruses are too small.","The virus cannot be cultured.","Gram stain is bacterial.","IF needs a specific antibody."],"k":"PCR: non-cultivable, low-copy viruses.","rn":[["","When is nucleic acid detection particularly helpful?","When is nucleic acid detection particularly helpful?","Non-cultivable viruses; Low copy numbers"],["","What is the commonest nucleic acid test?","What is the commonest nucleic acid test?","PCR"]]},{"id":35094,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Besides detecting a virus, what extra clinical information can PCR provide?","o":["The viral load","The Gram reaction of the virus","The LD50 of the strain in animals","The class of antibody the patient made","The shape of the virion"],"e":"PCR is quick, sensitive for low copy numbers, useful for non-cultivable viruses, and can quantify virus, i.e. the viral load.","w":["Viruses are not Gram stained.","LD50 is a research virulence measure.","That is serology.","Shape needs EM."],"k":"PCR can quantify viral load.","rn":[["","What can PCR quantify?","What can PCR quantify?","Viral load"]]},{"id":35095,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Name the nucleic acid test used on swabs for SARS-CoV-2.","a":["pcr","polymerase chain reaction","rt-pcr","rt pcr"],"e":"PCR (polymerase chain reaction) is the commonest viral nucleic acid test and was used for SARS-CoV-2 swabs.","k":"SARS-CoV-2 swab test = PCR.","rn":[["","What is the commonest nucleic acid test?","What is the commonest nucleic acid test?","PCR"]]},{"id":35096,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"An outbreak is caused by a virus for which no PCR assay exists. Which approach can characterise it?","o":["Culture in cells, EM of particles, then sequence the non-host genetic material","Gram stain the patient's blood and count the cocci","Run the random access analyser for known viruses","Use light microscopy to identify the virus by shape","Classify it using the Baltimore system from symptoms"],"e":"Electron microscopy is the one microscopy usable for viruses. It does not identify a virus alone, but for an unknown virus you can culture cells, see particles by EM, extract the non-host genetic material and sequence it. EM is not portable, so samples go to specialist centres.","w":["Gram stain is bacterial.","It only detects known viruses.","Light microscopy cannot resolve viruses.","Baltimore is not diagnostic."],"k":"Unknown virus: culture + EM + sequencing.","rn":[["","Why is electron microscopy useful for an unknown virus?","Why is electron microscopy useful for an unknown virus?","See particles; Can then extract genetic material and sequence"]]},{"id":35097,"t":"Viral Diagnosis","s":"Which test pairing is correct?","o":["ELISA: antibody in serum, about an hour","PCR: antibody in serum, several weeks","Immunofluorescence: nucleic acid by amplification","EM: routine screening for known viruses","Random access analyser: unknown viruses only"],"e":"Immunofluorescence detects antigen (quick); ELISA detects antibody (or antigen) in serum/plasma, about an hour even in the field; the random access analyser runs many serological tests in a couple of hours for suspected viruses; PCR detects nucleic acid within 24 h; EM is for unknown viruses.","w":["PCR detects nucleic acid within 24 h.","IF detects antigen.","EM is for unknown viruses.","The analyser tests known viruses."],"k":"Match each test to its target and speed.","rn":[["","What are the 4 layers of an ELISA, bottom to top?","What are the 4 layers of an ELISA, bottom to top?","Purified antigen on plate; Patient serum antibodies; Enzyme-coupled anti-human Ig (2nd antibody); Substrate that changes colour"],["","How quickly can ELISA be done in the field?","How quickly can ELISA be done in the field?","About an hour"],["","What is serology?","What is serology?","Antibody (or antigen) detection in serum/plasma"]]},{"id":35098,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"Which is a specific (rather than general) measure of viral prevention?","o":["Vaccination","Wearing gloves","Wearing masks","Social distancing","Hand hygiene"],"e":"Prevention measures are general (gloves, masks, isolation, social intervention, distancing, hygiene) and specific, long- or short-term (vaccines, chemoprophylaxis, immunoprophylaxis).","w":["General measure.","General measure.","General/social measure.","General measure."],"k":"Specific prevention: vaccines, chemo- and immunoprophylaxis.","rn":[["","What are 3 specific measures of viral prevention?","What are 3 specific measures of viral prevention?","Vaccines; Chemoprophylaxis; Immunoprophylaxis"]]},{"id":35099,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"Which statement about viral vaccines is supported by the lecture?","o":["They often attenuate illness rather than prevent infection","They always prevent any infection for life","Every important virus has a vaccine, including HIV","Influenza vaccine is given once in childhood only","Vaccines are made only from live viruses"],"e":"Vaccines inhibit the virus and reduce symptoms; they don't necessarily stop you getting ill but tend to attenuate illness, depending on the vaccine. Types: live (polio in the 1950s), killed and molecular subunit. There is no HIV vaccine; flu vaccine must be updated annually.","w":["Not always.","There is no HIV vaccine.","Flu vaccine is annual.","Killed and subunit vaccines exist too."],"k":"Vaccines often attenuate rather than prevent.","rn":[["","Do vaccines always stop you getting ill?","Do vaccines always stop you getting ill?","No, they attenuate illness"],["","Why do vaccinated people still sometimes get influenza?","Why do vaccinated people still sometimes get influenza?","Vaccine attenuates illness rather than blocking infection"],["","What is the main aim of vaccination?","What is the main aim of vaccination?","Stops you dying; Often does not stop illness"]]},{"id":35100,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"Why must the influenza vaccine be given every year?","o":["The virus continuously changes","Antibodies to it last only a week","It is a live vaccine that dies out","The vaccine stops working in winter","Influenza is a DNA virus"],"e":"Influenza vaccine has to be updated annually because the virus continuously changes. Influenza is a –ssRNA (Baltimore V) virus; RNA viruses mutate faster and adapt quickly.","w":["Not the reason given.","Not the reason given.","Not the reason given.","Influenza is an RNA virus."],"k":"Annual flu vaccine: the virus keeps changing.","rn":[["","Why must the influenza vaccine be updated annually?","Why must the influenza vaccine be updated annually?","Virus continuously changes"]]},{"id":35101,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"Which important virus has no vaccine available?","a":["hiv","human immunodeficiency virus","hiv-1"],"e":"Vaccines are available for MMR, HBV, HPV, varicella zoster, rotavirus and SARS-CoV-2, but not for some viruses such as HIV.","k":"No HIV vaccine.","rn":[["","Which virus has no vaccine?","Which virus has no vaccine?","HIV"]]},{"id":35102,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"Which vaccine types are listed?","o":["Live, killed and molecular subunit","Spore, capsule and LPS","Antibiotic, antifungal and antiviral","Serology, PCR and EM","Plasmid, transposon and phage"],"e":"Vaccine types: live viruses (e.g. polio in the 1950s), killed viruses and molecular subunits of the virus.","w":["Bacterial structures.","Drug classes.","Diagnostic tests.","Genetic elements."],"k":"Vaccines: live, killed, subunit.","rn":[["","Which 3 vaccine types exist?","Which 3 vaccine types exist?","Live viruses (polio, 1950s); Killed viruses; Molecular subunits"]]},{"id":35103,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"Which drug–virus pairing is correct?","o":["Aciclovir: HSV","Ganciclovir: influenza","Lamivudine: HSV","Tamiflu: hepatitis B virus","Ribavirin with PEG IFN: CMV"],"e":"Listed antivirals: HIV – HAART (not curative); HBV – lamivudine; HCV – ribavirin and PEG IFN; influenza – Tamiflu; HSV – aciclovir; CMV – ganciclovir; SARS-CoV-2 – PF-07321332/PAXLOVID, a viral protease inhibitor.","w":["Ganciclovir is for CMV.","Lamivudine is for HBV.","Tamiflu is for influenza.","That combination is for HCV."],"k":"HSV → aciclovir; CMV → ganciclovir.","rn":[["","What drug is used for HSV?","What drug is used for HSV?","Aciclovir"]]},{"id":35104,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"Name the antiviral listed for CMV.","a":["ganciclovir"],"e":"CMV: ganciclovir and others. HSV: aciclovir; influenza: Tamiflu; HBV: lamivudine; HCV: ribavirin with PEG IFN.","k":"CMV → ganciclovir.","rn":[["","What drug is used for cytomegalovirus (CMV) ?","What drug is used for cytomegalovirus (CMV) ?","Ganciclovir"]]},{"id":35105,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"What does HAART achieve in HIV infection?","o":["It keeps viral load to a minimum but does not cure","It cures HIV within weeks","It acts as a vaccine before exposure","It destroys latent virus in all cells","It is used only for hepatitis C"],"e":"HAART drugs keep HIV viral load to a minimum but are not curative. HIV is highlighted as an excellent example of translational research in antiviral therapy.","w":["Not curative.","HAART is treatment, not a vaccine.","Not stated.","It is for HIV."],"k":"HAART suppresses but does not cure HIV.","rn":[["","What do HAART drugs do in HIV?","What do HAART drugs do in HIV?","Keep viral load minimal; Not curative"]]},{"id":35106,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"What type of drug is PAXLOVID (PF-07321332) for SARS-CoV-2?","o":["A viral protease inhibitor","A live attenuated vaccine","A monoclonal antibody to ACE2","A pegylated interferon","A reverse transcriptase blocker for HIV"],"e":"PF-07321332/PAXLOVID is listed for SARS-CoV-2 as a viral protease inhibitor. Knowing what a virus does (Baltimore group, molecular interactions) lets drugs target one step and stop replication.","w":["It is a drug, not a vaccine.","Not stated.","PEG IFN is for HCV.","Not this drug."],"k":"PAXLOVID = SARS-CoV-2 protease inhibitor.","rn":[["","What does a viral protease inhibitor do?","What does a viral protease inhibitor do?","Blocks the viral protease (e.g. PAXLOVID for SARS-CoV-2)"],["","PAXLOVID is an example of what drug type?","PAXLOVID is an example of what drug type?","Viral protease inhibitor"]]},{"id":35107,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"Why are there relatively few antiviral drugs?","o":["Viruses use host machinery, so selective targets are hard to find","Viruses are too large for drugs to enter","All viruses are DNA viruses and drug resistant","Antivirals are never used clinically","Viruses have peptidoglycan that blocks drugs"],"e":"Generating antiviral therapy is complex and needs significant research. Viruses replicate inside cells using host machinery, so a drug must target a viral step (understood via Baltimore and molecular interactions) without harming the host.","w":["Viruses are tiny.","Many are RNA viruses.","Several antivirals are used.","Viruses lack peptidoglycan."],"k":"Antivirals must target viral steps without harming host.","rn":[["","How do viruses find their target cells?","How do viruses find their target cells?","Pure affinity; Random binding to receptors on cell surface"]]},{"id":35108,"t":"Viral Structure and Classification","s":"A virus is ssRNA, positive sense, with no DNA step. Its genome can be translated directly on entry. Which is it, from the examples?","o":["Poliovirus (Group IV)","Influenza virus (Group V)","Retrovirus (Group VI)","Reovirus (Group III)","Parvovirus (Group II)"],"e":"Group IV (+ssRNA) such as poliovirus have a genome that is already readable mRNA. Influenza is –ssRNA (V); retroviruses are +ssRNA with a DNA intermediate (VI); reovirus dsRNA (III); parvovirus ssDNA (II).","w":["Negative sense.","Has a DNA step.","Double-stranded RNA.","DNA virus."],"k":"+ssRNA, no DNA step = Group IV (polio).","rn":[["","What are the 7 Baltimore groups?","What are the 7 Baltimore groups?","I: dsDNA; II: ssDNA; III: dsRNA; IV: +ssRNA; V: -ssRNA; VI: +ssRNA with DNA intermediate; VII: gapped dsDNA"]]},{"id":35109,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Which pair is matched correctly?","o":["Active viraemia: measles","Passive viraemia: measles","Active viraemia: a mosquito bite before replication","Neural spread: hepatitis B","Pantropic: HPV"],"e":"Active viraemia results from replication (measles); passive from virus introduced without replication (dengue by mosquito). Neural spread: rabies, alpha herpesviruses. Pantropic: Ebola. HPV produces local lesions.","w":["Measles is active.","That is passive.","Not the neural example.","HPV causes local lesions."],"k":"Measles = active viraemia.","rn":[["","What is Active viremia?","What is Active viremia?","Presence of virus in the bloodstream; Results from virus replication; e.g. measles"]]},{"id":35110,"t":"Tropism and Virulence","s":"A virus has a single receptor found only on hepatocytes. Which prediction is most reasonable?","o":["Narrow tropism for the liver","It will be pantropic","It will be neurotropic","It cannot cause viraemia","It will be cleared by eyelid washing"],"e":"Tropism is set largely by receptors: cells lacking the receptor cannot be entered. A receptor restricted to one cell type gives narrow tropism; liver viruses can still cause active viraemia because the liver is highly vascular.","w":["Pantropic viruses infect many organs.","It needs neural receptors for that.","Liver infection can cause viraemia.","Irrelevant."],"k":"Restricted receptor → narrow tropism."},{"id":35111,"t":"Viral Prevention and Treatment","s":"Which statement summarises how a virus causes disease?","o":["It forces infected cells to make thousands of copies while evading defences","It divides by binary fission in blood","It releases endotoxin from its cell wall","It forms spores that germinate in tissues","It uses its own mitochondria to grow"],"e":"A virus needs living cells: an infected cell is forced to produce thousands of identical copies at an extraordinary rate. Viruses have evolved several ways to maintain pathogenicity and evade defences (e.g. virokines, viroceptors).","w":["Viruses do not divide.","Endotoxin is bacterial.","Spores are bacterial.","Viruses have no mitochondria."],"k":"Viruses hijack cells to mass-produce virions.","rn":[["","What does a virus force an infected host cell to do?","What does a virus force an infected host cell to do?","Make thousands of identical copies at extraordinary rate"]]},{"id":35112,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"Which transmission term fits a virus passing from bats to humans?","a":["zoonotic","zoonosis","zoonotic transmission"],"e":"Zoonotic transmission is between members of different species, e.g. from bats (a reservoir for many viruses) to humans. Horizontal is within a species.","k":"Bat → human = zoonotic.","rn":[["","What is Zoonotic transmission?","What is Zoonotic transmission?","Between members of different species"],["","Give an example of zoonotic transmission.","Give an example of zoonotic transmission.","Virus from bats ending up in humans"]]},{"id":35113,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"A hospital patient acquires a viral infection that spreads through the whole ward. Which term best describes infection acquired in a health care facility?","o":["Nosocomial","Germ line","Vertical","Zoonotic","Latent"],"e":"Nosocomial: infection while in hospital or a health care facility. Iatrogenic is narrower: a health worker's activity infecting a patient. Vertical is parent to offspring; germline via the genome; zoonotic between species.","w":["Via the genome.","Parent to offspring.","Between species.","Dormancy, not transmission."],"k":"Infection acquired in a facility = nosocomial.","rn":[["","What is Nosocomial transmission?","What is Nosocomial transmission?","Infected while in hospital or health care facility"]]},{"id":35114,"t":"Viral Spread","s":"Why might a virus remain localised in one person but spread systemically in another?","o":["The outcome depends on host defences as well as the virus","Viruses always behave identically in all hosts","Systemic spread needs an outer cell wall","Localised viruses lack nucleic acid","Systemic spread happens only in children"],"e":"A virus may stay local because it evolved for a particular site, or because the host's defences, though unable to stop entry, can contain it. Pathogenesis reflects the virus–host interplay.","w":["Host factors matter.","Viruses have no cell wall.","All viruses have nucleic acid.","Not stated."],"k":"Spread reflects virus–host interplay.","rn":[["","Why might a virus stay localised at the primary site?","Why might a virus stay localised at the primary site?","Evolved to remain in a particular cell site; Host defences cannot stop entry but contain it"]]},{"id":35115,"t":"Viral Pathogenesis and Transmission","s":"Rank these by typical size, smallest first: parvovirus, HIV-1, variola virus.","o":["Parvovirus < HIV-1 < variola","HIV-1 < parvovirus < variola","Variola < HIV-1 < parvovirus","Parvovirus < variola < HIV-1","All are the same size"],"e":"To scale: parvovirus 20 nm, HIV-1 120 nm, variola virus 360 nm. Ebolavirus is long (80 × 970 nm).","w":["Parvovirus is smallest.","Reversed.","Variola is larger than HIV-1.","They differ."],"k":"Parvovirus 20 nm < HIV-1 120 nm < variola 360 nm."}]);
